    "culture": {
      "text": {
        "id": 43,
        "title": "From Sovereigns to Servants by Alexandra Prokopenko review – Putin and his yes men",
        "source": "The Guardian - Culture",
        "source_id": "guardian_culture",
        "category": "culture",
        "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/nov/24/from-sovereigns-to-servants-by-alexandra-prokopenko-review-putin-and-his-yes-men",
        "published_at": "2026-11-24T09:00:11",
        "level": "C1",
        "slot_level": "C1",
        "source_level": "C1",
        "word_count": 668,
        "duration_sec": null,
        "body_text": "When Vladimir Putin launched his full-scale invasion of Ukraine four and a half years ago, it was a shock, but it wasn’t a surprise. The US and the UK had been publicly warning their allies for weeks about what Russia was planning, as well as threatening the Russians with a wave of sanctions unprecedented against a major power.",
        "audio_url": null,
        "description": null,
        "license": "proprietary",
        "attribution": null,
        "truncated": true,
        "guid_hash": "bbb58acf49be355800a52946a5eb8d949c4e3fc6",
        "summary": "A disturbing account of the way Russia’s elites have reduced themselves to craven courtiers When Vladimir Putin launched his full-scale invasion of Ukraine four and a half years ago, it was a shock, but it wasn’t a surprise. The US and the UK had been publicly warning their allies for weeks about what Russia was planning, as well as threatening the Russians with a wave of sanctions unprecedented against a major power. Putin’s foreign policy had long relied on confusion and untruth. By saying that they knew exactly what he was up to, Britain and America were hoping first to deny him his usual ambiguity, and second to pressure the men around him into making him stop, since war would deprive them of the stolen assets they’d stashed in London’s most exclusive neighbourhoods.",
        "origin": "fresh",
        "badges": [],
